```

Examples of consent sources:

```text
post_order_whatsapp_prompt
website_checkbox
qr_code_landing_page
restaurant_counter
campaign_landing_page
```

Do not permit restaurant owners to upload phone lists and claim consent. Alo King should require a verifiable first-party consent record.

Consent should be restaurant-scoped:

```text
restaurant_123 = opted in
restaurant_456 = muted
global_marketing = opted out
```

## 4. Immediate and layered opt-out

Support:

```text
STOP
UNSUBSCRIBE
إيقاف
ايقاف
وقف
توقف
إيقاف العروض
```

| Customer action | Result |
|---|---|
| **Mute this restaurant** | Stops promotions from one restaurant |
| **Stop restaurant offers** | Stops restaurant marketing while transactional messages continue |
| **STOP / global opt-out** | Stops all promotional WhatsApp messages from Alo King and restaurants |

Process STOP before normal routing and before any audience is generated. Re-check consent immediately before each delivery.

If a customer reports an unwanted message:

1. Opt them out immediately.
2. Stop future messages.
3. Flag the campaign and restaurant.
4. Record the complaint.
5. Require review before that restaurant sends again if complaints repeat.

## 5. Lebanon compliance posture

Lebanon’s Law No. 81/2018 requires consent for unsolicited electronic marketing in relevant circumstances and gives individuals a right to object to processing for commercial promotion. It also requires promotional advertisements to identify that they are promotional and identify the advertiser.

Alo King’s conservative operating rule should be:

> No promotional WhatsApp message without explicit, recorded, revocable consent that identifies the sender and the promotional purpose.

Every campaign should include:

- restaurant identification;
- clear promotional wording;
- a simple opt-out instruction;
- a way to contact Alo King;
- no misleading urgency or hidden conditions.

Have Lebanese counsel review the final Arabic consent text, retention periods, restaurant terms, and data-processing responsibilities.

## 6. Meta messaging limits

### Business portfolio limit

Meta’s messaging limit is based on the **business portfolio**, not independently per phone number. All Alo King business phone numbers in the same portfolio share the limit.

The limit is the maximum number of unique WhatsApp user phone numbers to whom the business can deliver messages outside a customer-service window during a moving 24-hour period.

New portfolios begin at:

```text
250 unique recipients
```

Possible higher tiers are:

```text
2,000
10,000
100,000
Unlimited
```

Adding additional restaurant numbers does not automatically create a separate quota for each restaurant.

### Scaling

Meta documents a path to 2,000 through business/partner verification or by delivering 2,000 messages outside customer-service windows to unique WhatsApp users within a moving 30-day period using high-quality templates.

Automatic scaling depends on:

- high-quality messages across the portfolio;
- using at least half of the current limit during the previous seven days;
- maintaining acceptable quality.

### Per-user marketing limits

Meta also applies adaptive marketing limits to individual users based on factors such as recent marketing read rate and overall inbox activity.

If error **131049** is received:

- mark the recipient temporarily marketing-suppressed;
- do not retry immediately;
- wait at least 24 hours before considering another marketing attempt;
- exclude the recipient from the active campaign as “not delivered due to user-level limit.”

## 7. Use stricter Alo King limits

Meta’s limits are a ceiling, not a recommended operating policy.

| Control | Initial Alo King rule |
|---|---:|
| Restaurant messages to one customer | Maximum 1 every 7 days |
| All restaurant promotions to one customer | Maximum 2 every 30 days |
| Quiet hours | 22:00–08:00 Asia/Beirut |
| New restaurant daily campaign | Start with 25–50 recipients |
| New template initial batch | 10–25 recipients |
| Campaign test period | 30–60 minutes before expansion |
| Portfolio concurrency | Keep below 50–70% of available quota |
| High opt-out campaign | Pause automatically |

## 8. Template-quality controls

Meta evaluates templates using negative feedback, read rate, and engagement.

```text
GREEN   high quality
YELLOW  medium quality
RED     low quality
UNKNOWN insufficient feedback
```

| Template state | Alo King action |
|---|---|
| GREEN | Normal controlled campaigns |
| UNKNOWN | Small test batch only |
| YELLOW | Reduce audience, improve copy, require review |
| RED | Stop using it and create a better variant |
| PAUSED | Block campaign immediately |

Meta may pace new, recently unpaused, or non-GREEN templates. Track:

```text
accepted
held_for_quality_assessment
sent
delivered
read
failed
```

## 9. Campaign rollout

### Phase 1 — Internal test

Use Alo King test numbers and verify Arabic/English copy, opt-out behavior, template status, delivery events, and billing webhooks.

### Phase 2 — Small test batch

Send to 10–25 recently active, opted-in customers. Monitor delivery, reads, replies, opt-outs, and complaints.

### Phase 3 — Controlled expansion

Expand only if delivery is healthy, no unusual complaint/block activity appears, the template remains GREEN, replies are handled, prices are accurate, and the restaurant can fulfil demand.

### Phase 4 — Full eligible audience

Send only after the test batch passes. Preserve a holdout group when possible.

## 10. Automatic kill switches

Pause immediately if:

- the template becomes YELLOW or RED;
- Meta sends a template-paused event;
- opt-outs or blocks spike;
- delivery failures rise sharply;
- the restaurant item becomes unavailable;
- the offer price changes;
- the restaurant cannot fulfil expected demand;
- portfolio quota is nearly exhausted;
- repeated 131049 errors occur;
- support workload becomes excessive.

