# Part II — Integrating AI recommendations with broadcasts

## 11. The core idea

Do not send the same promotion to every customer.

Instead, the campaign engine should generate a **campaign offer family**, and the recommendation engine should select the best eligible food suggestion for each recipient.

```text
Restaurant campaign objective
→ approved campaign offer family
→ eligible opted-in audience
→ customer profile and recent behavior
→ candidate menu items
→ hard safety and availability filters
→ personalized ranking
→ customer-specific message variables
→ controlled send
→ reply/cart/order attribution
```

The AI should personalize the **relevance and wording**, not the legal or financial controls.

## 12. Example personalized campaign

Restaurant campaign objective:

```text
Reactivate customers who have not ordered for 30+ days
```

Approved offer family:

```text
Family meal for 4
Price: $24
Available in: Baakline, Beiteddine
Valid: Thursday–Sunday
```

Customer A:

```text
Previously ordered: chicken shawarma
Usually orders: Friday evening
Typical basket: $18–$25
Language: Lebanese Arabic
```

Customer B:

```text
Previously ordered: vegetarian platter
Usually orders: Sunday lunch
Language: English
```

The system may send different approved variants:

### Customer A

> اشتقتوا للشاورما؟ عرض عيلة من **[Restaurant Name]** بيكفي 4 أشخاص بـ **$24**. متوفر للتوصيل اليوم ببلدتكم. اطلبوا هلّق.

### Customer B

> Your usual vegetarian-style meal from **[Restaurant Name]** is back. Try the family platter for four for **$24**, available for delivery today in your area.

Both messages must be generated from the same approved offer, valid price, delivery area, and template constraints.

## 13. Personalization signals

Use these signals in priority order:

1. Explicit dietary/allergen constraints.
2. Restaurant-specific consent and mute state.
3. Current delivery village and serviceability.
4. Recent successful orders.
5. Frequently ordered dishes.
6. Time of day and ordering day.
7. Basket pairings.
8. Price range.
9. Previous campaign engagement.
10. Controlled exploration of new items.

Do not infer sensitive health, religion, or financial traits from food orders.

## 14. Candidate generation

The campaign should not ask the LLM to invent a dish. Generate candidates from the restaurant’s live menu:

```sql
select item_id, name, description, price_usd, available,
       delivery_villages, category, customization_options
from menu_items
where vendor_id = :vendor_id
  and available = true;
```

Candidate items should also satisfy:

- current inventory;
- current price;
- restaurant opening hours;
- delivery village;
- campaign validity dates;
- minimum order rules;
- allergy and diet constraints;
- campaign offer eligibility.

## 15. Personalized ranking formula

Use a deterministic ranker such as:

```text
score(item, customer, campaign) =
  hard_filter(item, customer, campaign)
  × (
    0.30 × request_or_campaign_fit
  + 0.20 × recent_preference_fit
  + 0.15 × reorder_probability
  + 0.10 × basket_pairing_fit
  + 0.10 × time_and_occasion_fit
  + 0.05 × price_fit
  + 0.05 × restaurant_reliability
  + 0.05 × controlled_exploration
  )
```

The exact weights should be versioned and changed only through experiments.

Store the reasons:

```json
{
  "customer_id_hash": "cust_abc",
  "campaign_id": "camp_123",
  "selected_item_id": "item_456",
  "ranker_version": "broadcast_ranker_v1",
  "reasons": [
    "ordered_similar_item_14_days_ago",
    "within_customer_price_range",
    "available_in_village",
    "matches_friday_evening_pattern"
  ]
}
```

## 16. Use the LLM for language, not truth

The LLM can:

- translate approved copy into Lebanese Arabic or English;
- select a tone from approved options;
- explain why the suggestion is relevant;
- create short copy variants;
- interpret a customer reply;
- answer questions about the offer using verified tool data.

The LLM must not:

- invent prices;
- invent discounts;
- claim an item is available without a tool result;
- recommend an item that violates an explicit allergy;
- override consent or suppression;
- choose recipients outside the approved audience;
- send a campaign without merchant confirmation;
- change a campaign budget;
- make a prohibited health or dietary claim.

## 17. Personalization levels

Use a fallback ladder so every customer receives a valid message even when data is sparse:

### Level 0 — No history

Use a broadly relevant best seller or campaign item, subject to availability and consent.

### Level 1 — Restaurant history

Recommend a previously ordered item or a compatible alternative.

### Level 2 — Basket history

Add a complementary product based on previous baskets.

### Level 3 — Timing and location

Adjust item and wording for village, delivery feasibility, weekday, and meal period.

### Level 4 — Explicit preferences

Use explicit language, diet, spice, ingredient, and budget preferences.

### Level 5 — Campaign learning

Use prior campaign reads, replies, carts, and completed orders to improve future selection.

Do not fabricate personalization when the customer has no history.

## 18. Campaign message structure

Keep the message short and useful:

```text
[Personal relevance]
[Restaurant + approved offer]
[Price and validity]
[One clear CTA]
[Opt-out instruction]
```

Example:

> Your usual shawarma spot has a family bundle for 4 at **$24** this Friday. Tap below to order from **[Restaurant Name]**. Reply STOP to stop restaurant offers.

Use one primary call to action:

```text
Order now
View bundle
Remind me later
Mute this restaurant
```

A “mute restaurant” action should not be treated as a global STOP.

## 19. Replies should enter a campaign-aware bot flow

When a customer replies, pass the campaign context into the bot:

```text
campaign_id
restaurant_id
offer_id
selected_item_id
message_language
customer_memory_snapshot
```

The bot should then:

- answer offer questions;
- explain availability and delivery;
- build the cart;
- preserve the campaign price if still valid;
- revalidate price and inventory before checkout;
- apply the correct campaign attribution;
- request final order confirmation.

Example:

```text
Customer: “Can I make it spicy and add extra toum?”
Bot: validates customization and current price
→ updates cart
→ shows new total
→ asks for confirmation
```

## 20. Attribution and learning loop

Connect every campaign recipient to:

```text
campaign
→ recipient
→ message status
→ reply
→ item click
→ cart
→ order
→ completed delivery
→ revenue
```

Use a holdout group to measure incremental impact.

Important metrics:

- delivered-message rate;
- read rate;
- reply rate;
- opt-out rate;
- cart-start rate;
- completed-order rate;
- average order value;
- incremental orders versus holdout;
- revenue per delivered message;
- campaign cost;
- incremental profit;
- repeat-order rate.

The most important commercial metric is:

```text
Incremental completed orders ÷ campaign cost
```

